{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.source_spin_sequence_limit","record":{"id":"scalar.source_spin_sequence_limit","kind":"claim","label":"Actual convergence of the normalized expected finite-spin maximum","status":"unverified","source_id":"scalar-r2-13-source-spin-limit","locator":"GroundState/BlockCoeff.lean:237–263; SKValueG.groundStateSequence_tendsto","statement":"Tendsto groundStateSequence atTop (nhds groundStateValue). The statement has no W, gamma, minimizer, or diffusion argument.","assumption_ids":[],"evidence_ids":[],"citation_ids":["scalar-r2-13-cite-spin-sequence-limit"],"boundary":"Proof uses expectedMaximum_superadditive at117–123 and expectedMaximum_upper at210–235. This is convergence of expectations, not an almost-sure finite-system optimum theorem; limUnder by itself would not establish it. Authored correspondence to inspected frozen source; unverified status is retained. No fresh Lean build, elaborated dependency extraction, axiom audit, Comparator acceptance, or independent mathematical acceptance is claimed."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.source_spin_sequence_limit","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/2f85413164f06365ea0d825befee447650da71d54080d5dd65e78a708891c560","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:cfbe950801fd334d8fd957243fa3b1f716ef609148c5f2ff97d44f9a6443d82c/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]},{"stage":"expository","subgraph_ref":"sha256:479155de953858e60f1454491ccda2215d88a4c2780e686cdfb8422481c62d9c/papers/expository.json/support/0.3.1/9dd5536bc567fc55efaa864733bf89747cea0268a8a3865dec51ddd8e2a235dd","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/formal-lower-overview","anchor":"formal-lower-overview","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/formal-lower-overview","anchor":"formal-lower-overview","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n<a id=\"independent-scalar-lower-bound\"></a>\n\n### 10.2 A universal bound for predictable correlations\n\nThe scalar implementation obtains the value equality by two inequalities.\nIts finite-spin limit follows from superadditivity and a uniform upper\nbound. A finite-profile Gaussian comparison supplies\n$P_*\\le\\mathcal P(\\gamma)$ for every admissible coefficient. The reverse\ninequality at a minimizer comes from a more general finite-dimensional\nfact: predictable orthonormal Gaussian functions paired with any spin\nhave a shifted correlation sum at most $P_*$. We reconstruct that fact\nbelow. It supplies the inequality that §7's mesh limit consumes, without\ninvoking the literature's Parisi formula used in §6.\n\nWrite $e_n=n^{-1}\\mathbb E\\max_\\sigma H_n(\\sigma)$, with the Hamiltonian\nof §1. The finite-spin convergence and upper comparison are separate\ninputs to this subsection; their precise suppliers are\n[the superadditive limit][scalar-spin-limit] and\n[the upper comparison][scalar-upper]. The proof below concerns the\nlower comparison, not a finite-disorder algorithm or a circuit.\n\n**Proposition 10.1 (predictable Gaussian comparison).** Fix $K<\\infty$, independent standard normals $z_0,\\ldots,z_K$,\nand real functions $f_j=f_j(z_0,\\ldots,z_j)$ for $0\\le j<K$.\nAssume measurability, square integrability and\n$\\mathbb E[f_if_j]=\\delta_{ij}$. For any measurable\n$S=S(z_0,\\ldots,z_K)\\in\\{-1,1\\}$, the conclusion is\n$$\n\\sum_{j=0}^{K-1}\\mathbb E[f_jS]\\,\\mathbb E[S z_{j+1}]\\le P_*.\n$$\nThese assumptions allow nonconstant, dependent $f_j$; even\n$\\mathbb E f_j=0$ is unnecessary. Their order of dependence ensures\n$\\mathbb E[f_i z_{j+1}]=0$ whenever $i\\le j$. The next fresh coordinate,\nrather than centring of $f_i$, is what will remove the projection terms.\n[Scalar implementation, predictable-coefficient theorem][scalar-predictable].\n","span":{"start_byte":181678,"end_byte":183522,"start_line":3879,"end_line":3913},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=formal-lower-overview","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=formal-lower-overview"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_spin_sequence_limit&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_spin_sequence_limit&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=scalar.source_spin_sequence_limit&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=scalar.source_spin_sequence_limit&relation=prerequisites